+# Holds
+
+Status: ready-for-agent
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+When every copy of a title is out, a member who wants it can only keep asking at the desk. Nobody keeps track of who asked first, so a returned copy goes back on the shelf for whoever happens to walk past, and the members who wanted it most are no better off for having asked.
+
+## Solution
+
+A member can place a **hold** on a title that has no copy on the shelf, joining the title's **queue**. When a copy of that title comes back, it is **set aside** for the hold at the front of the queue, which becomes **ready**. The member has a **pickup time** (7 days, never ending on a Sunday) to **collect** it by borrowing the set-aside copy in the usual way. A daily job **expires** ready holds whose pickup time has passed, charges the member a **no-show fee**, and passes the copy on to the next hold in the queue, or back to the shelf. Members can cancel a hold at any time at no cost. A member owing 5.00 or more is **blocked** from placing holds.
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a member, I want to place a hold on a title when every copy is out, so that I get the next copy without having to keep asking.
+2. As a librarian, I want a hold refused when a copy is on the shelf, so that members take that copy instead of queueing for it.
+3. As a librarian, I want a hold refused on a title with no copies in circulation (none at all, or all withdrawn), so that nobody waits for a copy that will never come back.
+4. As a librarian, I want a member to have at most one active hold per title, so that nobody holds two places in one queue.
+5. As a librarian, I want a member who has a copy of a title on loan refused a hold on it, so that a returned copy is never set aside for the person who just returned it.
+6. As a librarian, I want a member owing 5.00 or more blocked from placing holds, so that members settle up before claiming copies other people want.
+7. As a librarian, I want a member owing 4.99 still able to place a hold, so that the threshold is exact.
+8. As a librarian, I want a blocked member told they are blocked even when the hold would also be refused for another reason, so that the more important problem is reported first, as at checkout.
+9. As a librarian, I want children blocked from holds at the same threshold as adults, so that blocking stays the same for every category.
+10. As a librarian, I want to list the active holds on a title in queue order, so that I can tell a member where they stand.
+11. As a librarian, I want a returned copy set aside for the first member in the queue, so that the earliest hold is served first.
+12. As a librarian, I want a returned copy with nobody waiting to go back on the shelf as before, so that returns without holds don't change.
+13. As a librarian, I want a late return that is set aside to be fined exactly as before, so that holds don't change fines.
+14. As a member with a ready hold, I want to collect it by borrowing the set-aside copy at the desk as normal, so that collecting is just a checkout.
+15. As a librarian, I want anyone else trying to borrow a set-aside copy refused, so that the copy really waits for its member.
+16. As a librarian, I want a member collecting a hold still subject to the borrowing block and the loan limit, so that a hold is never a way around them.
+17. As a member, I want 7 days to collect a ready hold, and never a pickup-by date on a Sunday, so that I have a fair chance to get to the library.
+18. As a librarian, I want a daily job that expires ready holds whose pickup-by date has passed, so that uncollected copies don't sit on the hold shelf.
+19. As a librarian, I want an expired hold to charge the member a 1.00 no-show fee, so that not collecting a set-aside copy has a cost.
+20. As a parent, I want my child never charged a no-show fee, so that children are treated as they are for fines.
+21. As a librarian, I want the copy from an expired hold set aside for the next member in the queue, with a fresh pickup time, or put back on the shelf if nobody is waiting, so that copies keep moving.
+22. As a librarian, I want waiting holds never to expire, however long they wait, so that patient members keep their place.
+23. As a librarian, I want a member who turns up late but before the job has run still able to collect, without a fee, so that the desk never refuses a hold the system still shows as ready.
+24. As a librarian, I want the daily job to catch up if it missed days, so that a skipped run doesn't leave stale holds behind.
+25. As a librarian, I want the daily job to tell me which holds it expired, so that I can clear them off the hold shelf.
+26. As a member, I want to cancel a hold, waiting or ready, at no charge, so that I can let the next person have the copy.
+27. As a librarian, I want a cancelled ready hold's copy passed on exactly as an expired one's would be, so that cancelling and expiring move copies the same way.
+28. As a member with a hold, I want borrowing any copy of that title (for example a new copy just added to the shelf) to fulfil my hold, so that I don't keep a pointless place in the queue.
+29. As a developer, I want unknown members, titles and holds to raise the same not-found errors as elsewhere, and every new failure to be a lending error, so that error handling stays uniform.
+30. As a developer, I want the hold numbers (blocking threshold, no-show fee, pickup time) kept with the other circulation policy, so that they live in one place.
+31. As a librarian, I want a refused hold to leave nothing behind, so that a failed request never takes a place in the queue.
+32. As a librarian, I want each no-show fee recorded with a reason naming the hold and the copy, so that I can explain the fee to the member.
+33. As a librarian, I want no-show fees to count towards both the borrowing and the hold blocking thresholds, so that they work like any other charge.
+34. As a librarian, I want a set-aside copy left out of the available copies and impossible to withdraw, so that nobody takes it off the hold shelf by mistake.
+35. As a developer, I want the daily job to take today from the injected clock and be safe to run more than once a day, so that whatever schedules it can stay simple.
+36. As a developer, I want `return_copy` and `checkout` to keep their signatures and return values, so that existing callers keep working.
+37. As a developer, I want the queue to tell waiting holds apart from ready ones, so that renewals can later be refused while anyone is still waiting.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+**Public interface (fixed; other code will call exactly this), on `LendingService`:**
+
+- `place_hold(member_id: str, isbn: str) -> Hold`
+- `cancel_hold(hold_id: str) -> Hold`
+- `expire_holds() -> list[Hold]`: the daily job. Returns the holds it expired.
+- `holds_for(isbn: str) -> list[Hold]`: active (waiting and ready) holds on the title, in queue order.
+- `return_copy(copy_id)` and `checkout(member_id, copy_id)` keep their signatures and return values.
+
+**Models:**
+
+- `Hold(hold_id: str, member_id: str, isbn: str, placed_on: date, status: HoldStatus, copy_id: str | None, pickup_by: date | None)`. A plain mutable dataclass, changed only by the service.
+- `HoldStatus`: `WAITING`, `READY`, `FULFILLED`, `CANCELLED`, `EXPIRED`. Active means `WAITING` or `READY`.
+- `CopyStatus.ON_HOLD_SHELF`: a copy set aside for a ready hold.
+- `EntryKind.NO_SHOW_FEE`: a new automatic charge kind.
+- Hold ids come from the repository's sequence (`H0001`, `H0002`, ...), so queue order is placement order: earliest `placed_on` first, then placement sequence.
+- `Hold` and `HoldStatus` are exported from the package alongside the other models.
+
+**Errors (subclasses of `LendingError`):**
+
+- `HoldNotAllowed`: a hold can't be placed, or a hold that is no longer active can't be cancelled. Carries a short `reason`.
+- `HoldNotFound`: carries `hold_id`, mirroring `LoanNotFound`.
+- Existing errors are reused where they fit: `MemberNotFound`, `TitleNotFound`, `AccountBlocked` (with the hold threshold), `CopyNotAvailable`.
+
+**Placing a hold.** `place_hold` checks in this order, the same order `checkout` uses:
+
+1. `MemberNotFound`
+2. `AccountBlocked`: balance ≥ the hold blocking threshold of 5.00, the same for every category.
+3. `TitleNotFound`
+4. `HoldNotAllowed`, when any of these is true:
+   - the title has no copy that isn't withdrawn;
+   - some copy of the title is `AVAILABLE`;
+   - the member already has an active hold on the title;
+   - the member has a copy of the title on loan.
+
+Copies on loan and copies set aside for someone else both count as out. A new hold is `WAITING` with `placed_on` = today, and `copy_id` and `pickup_by` both `None`.
+
+**Setting aside on return.** After closing the loan and charging any fine exactly as now:
+
+- If the title has waiting holds, the copy becomes `ON_HOLD_SHELF`. The first waiting hold becomes `READY`, with `copy_id` set and `pickup_by` = the pickup-by date counted from today.
+- Otherwise the copy becomes `AVAILABLE` as before.
+
+**Pickup-by date.** The day the copy was set aside + 7 calendar days, moved to the next open day if it lands on a closed day (the same shape as `due_date`). A ready hold can be collected through its pickup-by date, and in fact until the daily job expires it: `checkout` never looks at `pickup_by`.
+
+**Passing a copy on.** One rule, used by expiry, by cancelling a ready hold, and by fulfilling a ready hold with a different copy:
+
+- The copy is set aside for the first waiting hold on its title, with a pickup-by date counted from today.
+- With nobody waiting, it becomes `AVAILABLE`.
+
+**Collecting, and checkout generally.** `checkout` keeps its order: `MemberNotFound` → `AccountBlocked` (borrowing threshold 10.00) → `CopyNotFound` → `CopyNotAvailable` → `LoanLimitReached`.
+
+- An `ON_HOLD_SHELF` copy counts as available only to the member whose ready hold it is set aside for. Anyone else gets `CopyNotAvailable`.
+- On a successful checkout, if the member has an active hold on that title:
+  - the hold becomes `FULFILLED`, and its `copy_id` records the copy actually borrowed;
+  - a waiting hold fulfilled this way keeps `pickup_by` = `None`;
+  - if the hold was ready on a *different* copy, that copy is passed on.
+- A refused checkout changes no hold.
+
+**Expiry (`expire_holds`).** For every `READY` hold with `pickup_by` before today, in queue order:
+
+- the hold becomes `EXPIRED`;
+- a no-show fee of 1.00 is charged, unless the member's category pays no automatic charges (children). The fee's reason names the hold and the copy, e.g. "uncollected hold H0001 (D1)";
+- the copy is passed on.
+
+`WAITING` holds never expire. Running the job again the same day expires nothing new. The fee is a dated entry made when the hold expires, not something that builds up day by day, which is in line with ADR-0001.
+
+**Cancelling (`cancel_hold`).** `HoldNotFound` for an unknown id. A `WAITING` or `READY` hold becomes `CANCELLED`; if it was ready, its copy is passed on. No fee is charged. A hold that is already fulfilled, cancelled or expired raises `HoldNotAllowed`.
+
+**Listing (`holds_for`).** `TitleNotFound` for an unknown ISBN. Returns the waiting and ready holds in queue order; a ready hold is where it was in the queue, at the front.
+
+**Policy:** The hold blocking threshold (5.00), the no-show fee (1.00), the pickup time (7 days) and the pickup-by date calculation live in circulation policy, next to the borrowing blocking threshold and `due_date`. The child exemption reuses the existing "does this member category pay automatic charges" rule.
+
+**Repository:** stores holds and gives back one hold by id (raising `HoldNotFound`) and a title's holds in placement order. "The waiting holds on a title" is a single query, used both for setting aside and later for renewals (see Further Notes).
+
+**Unchanged:** `withdraw_copy` still only withdraws `AVAILABLE` copies, so a set-aside copy can't be withdrawn. `add_copy` still shelves new copies as `AVAILABLE` even when a queue exists. `available_copies` excludes set-aside copies. The overdue report is unchanged. The README's Concepts section gains lines on holds and the `ON_HOLD_SHELF` copy status.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam: the public methods of `LendingService`. No new seams.**
+  - Placing, cancelling and listing go through `place_hold`, `cancel_hold` and `holds_for`.
+  - Setting aside and collecting go through the existing `return_copy` and `checkout`, with the results read back through `get_copy`, `available_copies` and `holds_for`.
+  - Balances are set up with `add_charge` and `pay`, and no-show fees are checked with `balance`.
+  - Tests assert only on what these methods return or raise.
+- **The daily job is tested by calling `expire_holds()` directly and moving the `FixedClock`** (`set`/`advance`) to control "today". Nothing models a scheduler; running the job daily is up to whatever calls it.
+- **A good test describes behaviour a librarian would recognise,** for example "a copy set aside on Sun 8 Mar can be collected until Mon 16 Mar", not "the pickup-by function returns X". Tests don't reach into the repository, the account entries or the policy functions.
+- **Prior art:** the existing blocking and fines tests. Use the shared `service` and `clock` fixtures (`FixedClock` starting Mon 2 Mar 2026; members `ann` (adult) and `kid` (child); book `978-1` with copies B1 and B2; DVD `dvd-1` with the single copy D1, replacement cost 12.00). Register extra adult members inside tests where a queue needs more than one person. New tests live in their own module.
+- **Scenarios to cover:**
+  - `ann` borrows D1 on Mon 2 Mar; `bob` places a hold on `dvd-1`. It's `WAITING`, with `placed_on` 2 Mar and no copy or pickup-by date.
+  - Refusals:
+    - a hold on `978-1` while B1 or B2 is on the shelf: `HoldNotAllowed`;
+    - a title whose only copy is withdrawn: `HoldNotAllowed`;
+    - a second hold by the same member: `HoldNotAllowed`;
+    - `ann` holding D1 places a hold on `dvd-1`: `HoldNotAllowed`.
+  - Blocking: owing 5.00 gives `AccountBlocked` with threshold 5.00; owing 4.99 is allowed; a child owing 5.00 is blocked; a blocked member asking for a title that has a copy on the shelf gets `AccountBlocked`.
+  - `MemberNotFound` / `TitleNotFound` from `place_hold`; `TitleNotFound` from `holds_for`; `HoldNotFound` from `cancel_hold`.
+  - `ann` returns D1 on Thu 5 Mar with `bob` waiting:
+    - D1 is `ON_HOLD_SHELF` and not in `available_copies`;
+    - `bob`'s hold is `READY` with `copy_id` D1 and `pickup_by` Thu 12 Mar;
+    - `return_copy` returns the closed loan.
+  - A copy returned on Sun 8 Mar (book drop) gets pickup-by Mon 16 Mar, not Sun 15 Mar.
+  - A late return that is set aside is still fined.
+  - Collecting:
+    - `bob` checks out D1 and the hold is `FULFILLED`;
+    - `carol` checking out D1 gets `CopyNotAvailable`;
+    - `bob` owing 10.00 gets `AccountBlocked` and the hold stays `READY`.
+  - Queue order: `bob` then `carol` hold `dvd-1`, and `holds_for` lists `bob` first. After the return, `bob` is ready and `carol` is still waiting.
+  - Expiry:
+    - the job on Thu 12 Mar expires nothing;
+    - the job on Fri 13 Mar expires `bob`'s hold, returns it, and `bob`'s balance is 1.00;
+    - with `carol` waiting, D1 is set aside for her with pickup-by Fri 20 Mar; with nobody waiting, D1 is `AVAILABLE`;
+    - a second run the same day returns `[]`;
+    - a child's expired hold leaves their balance at 0;
+    - a waiting hold placed months ago never expires;
+    - `bob` collecting on Sat 14 Mar before any job has run succeeds with no fee.
+  - Cancelling: a waiting hold → `CANCELLED`, and it's gone from `holds_for`. A ready hold → `CANCELLED` with no fee, and its copy passes to the next waiting hold or the shelf. Cancelling it again: `HoldNotAllowed`.
+  - Other copy: `bob` waiting on `dvd-1`; staff `add_copy("D2", "dvd-1")`; `bob` checks out D2. His hold is `FULFILLED` with `copy_id` D2. Same for a ready hold on D1: D1 passes to the next in the queue.
+- The existing test suite must keep passing unchanged.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Setting newly added copies aside for a queue (`add_copy` shelves them as `AVAILABLE`).
+- Expiring or tidying up waiting holds that can never be served because every copy was withdrawn; staff cancel them by hand.
+- A limit on the number of holds a member may have; holds don't count towards the loan limit.
+- Notifying members that a hold is ready.
+- Who is allowed to cancel a hold (no authorisation in the service, as elsewhere).
+- **Renewals** (next feature): see Further Notes.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- Vocabulary (Hold, Queue, Set aside, Ready, Pickup time, Collect, Expire, plus No-show fee, Blocking threshold, Blocked) is in `GLOSSARY.md`. "Limit" still means open loans; the 5.00 is the hold **blocking threshold**.
+- The 5.00 hold blocking threshold and the 1.00 no-show fee (child exemption, counting towards the blocks) were settled in the overdue-fines spec (its Out of Scope section).
+- **Renewals, for later; the holds design must keep them easy:**
+  - (1) While any hold on a title is `WAITING`, loans of that title can't be renewed. A `READY` hold doesn't count, because that member already has their copy. The status alone tells the two apart, and the repository's "waiting holds on a title" query answers this directly.
+  - (2) Renewals are only for members who are paid up: any balance above 0 when they ask blocks renewal until they pay. In this glossary's terms, that's a renewal blocking threshold of 0.01, raised as `AccountBlocked`. The check uses the balance as it stands when the member asks.
+  - Open until renewals are designed: by ADR-0001, a fine building up on an overdue loan isn't part of that balance. So a member who owes nothing could renew an overdue loan, and the renewal would then charge the fine (see the renewals note in the overdue-fines spec).
