@@ -0,0 +1,137 @@
+# Overdue fines and member accounts
+
+Status: ready-for-agent
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Returning a copy late costs a member nothing, so DVDs routinely come back weeks after their due date and other members wait for them. Staff have nowhere to record money a member owes, whether for lateness or for anything else (a damaged case, a lost insert), no way to record that a member has paid, and no way to stop a member who owes a lot from walking out with more copies.
+
+## Solution
+
+Every member gets an **account**. When a copy comes back late, a **fine** is charged to the member's account automatically, priced per open day late by material and never more, in total for one loan, than the title's replacement cost. Children are never fined. Staff can add a **manual charge** with a reason, members can make a **payment**, and anyone can ask for a member's **balance**. A member whose balance is at or above the borrowing **blocking threshold** (10.00) is **blocked** from borrowing until they pay it down.
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a librarian, I want a late return to charge the member a fine automatically, so that lateness has a cost without anyone having to remember to charge it.
+2. As a librarian, I want the late return itself to behave exactly as before (the loan is closed, the copy goes back on the shelf, the same loan is handed back), so that the desk workflow does not change.
+3. As a librarian, I want DVDs fined at 1.00 per late day, so that the material that comes back latest carries the stronger incentive.
+4. As a librarian, I want books fined at 0.25 per late day, so that book lateness is discouraged without being punitive.
+5. As a member, I want only open days counted towards my fine, so that I am not charged for a Sunday when the library was closed and I could not return the copy.
+6. As a member, I want the day I return the copy to count as a late day if the library is open that day, so that the rule is easy to understand: every open day after the due date up to and including the return day.
+7. As a member who drops a copy in the book drop on a Sunday the day after it was due, I want no fine, so that I am not charged when no open day has passed.
+8. As a member, I want a copy returned on or before its due date to cost nothing, so that on-time returns are never charged.
+9. As a member, I want no grace period surprises: one open day late is charged, so that the rule is predictable.
+10. As a member, I want the fines on one loan never to exceed the replacement cost of the title, so that a very late return never costs more than replacing the copy.
+11. As a parent, I want my child never to be fined for a late return, so that children are not put off borrowing.
+12. As a librarian, I want a child's late return processed normally (loan closed, copy shelved) with nothing charged, so that the only difference for children is the money.
+13. As a librarian, I want a return that costs nothing to leave no trace on the account, so that accounts are not cluttered with zero-value entries.
+14. As a librarian, I want each fine recorded with the loan it came from, the due date it was counted from, the day it was charged and a reason such as "late return of D1", so that I can explain any fine to the member.
+15. As a librarian, I want to add a manual charge to a member's account with a reason, so that I can bill for damage or other losses.
+16. As a librarian, I want adding a manual charge to tell me the member's new balance, so that I can tell the member straight away what they owe.
+17. As a librarian, I want to add a manual charge to a child's account, so that damage by a child can still be billed even though children are not fined.
+18. As a librarian, I want the reason I type for a manual charge to be stored exactly as I typed it, so that the account says what I meant.
+19. As a member, I want to pay some or all of what I owe, so that I can reduce my balance.
+20. As a librarian, I want recording a payment to tell me the member's new balance, so that I can confirm it at the desk.
+21. As a librarian, I want a payment larger than the balance refused, so that members never end up in credit and I never have to hand money back.
+22. As a librarian, I want a payment when the member owes nothing refused, so that stray payments are not recorded.
+23. As a librarian, I want to ask what a member owes right now, so that I can tell them before they borrow.
+24. As a librarian, I want a member's balance to be all charges minus all payments, so that it always matches the entries on the account.
+25. As a member who has never been charged, I want my balance to be zero, so that new and well-behaved members start clean.
+26. As a librarian, I want a member whose balance is 10.00 or more blocked from borrowing, so that members who owe a lot settle up before taking more copies.
+27. As a librarian, I want a member owing 9.99 still able to borrow, so that the threshold is exact and predictable.
+28. As a member who is blocked, I want to be able to borrow again as soon as a payment brings my balance below 10.00, so that paying immediately restores my borrowing.
+29. As a librarian, I want the blocked error to tell me the member, their balance and the threshold, so that I can explain the refusal.
+30. As a librarian, I want a blocked member told they are blocked even if the copy they picked is also unavailable, so that the more important problem is reported first.
+31. As a librarian, I want children blocked at the same threshold as adults, so that a child with large manual charges is treated like anyone else.
+32. As a librarian, I want amounts that cannot be money (zero, negative, fractions of a penny, not-a-number, infinity, floating-point values) refused for both charges and payments, so that the account never holds a nonsense amount.
+33. As a developer, I want whole-number amounts accepted as money, so that callers can pass a plain integer for whole amounts.
+34. As a developer, I want asking about, charging or paying for an unknown member to raise the same "member not found" error as everywhere else, so that error handling stays uniform.
+35. As a developer, I want every new failure to be a lending error, so that callers that catch lending errors keep working.
+36. As a developer, I want the overdue report unchanged, so that existing front-desk printouts keep working.
+37. As a developer, I want the fine calculation to depend only on the due date, the day lateness is settled, the material and what has already been charged on the loan, so that renewals can reuse it later without restructuring.
+38. As a developer, I want the circulation numbers for fines and blocking (daily rates, the borrowing blocking threshold, the child exemption) kept with the other circulation policy, so that they live in one place.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+**Public interface (fixed; other code will call exactly this), all on `LendingService`:**
+
+- `return_copy(copy_id)`: signature and return value unchanged (returns the closed `Loan`). In addition, a late return charges the fine to the member's account.
+- `balance(member_id: str) -> Decimal`: what the member owes right now.
+- `add_charge(member_id: str, amount: Decimal, reason: str) -> Decimal`: records a manual charge and returns the new balance.
+- `pay(member_id: str, amount: Decimal) -> Decimal`: records a payment and returns the new balance.
+- No other new public methods on the service. In particular, there is no method to read account history yet.
+
+**Errors (added to the lending errors module, both subclasses of `LendingError`):**
+
+- `AccountBlocked`: raised by `checkout` when the member is blocked from borrowing. It carries `member_id`, `balance` and `threshold` so the same error can later be reused for holds with a different threshold.
+- `InvalidAmount`: raised by `add_charge` and `pay` for amounts that cannot be accepted. It carries the rejected `amount`.
+- Unknown members keep raising `MemberNotFound` from `balance`, `add_charge` and `pay`. The member is looked up before the amount is validated.
+
+**Fines (see ADR-0001: fines are charged when lateness is settled, never accrued):**
+
+- A fine is charged only at return. Fines building up on open overdue loans are not part of the balance and do not block.
+- Late days are the open days strictly after the due date, up to and including the return day. Closed days (Sundays) never count. There is no grace period.
+- Daily rate by material: DVD 1.00, book 0.25. Fine = late days × rate.
+- Per-loan limit: the total of all fines on one loan never exceeds the title's replacement cost. The fine is calculated as the smaller of (late days × rate) and (replacement cost minus fines already charged on that loan). Today a loan only ever gets one fine, but calculating it this way means renewals inherit the right behaviour.
+- Children pay no automatic charges. A child's late return closes the loan and shelves the copy as normal, and nothing is charged. The exemption is one policy rule ("does this member category pay automatic charges"), not a per-category rate factor.
+- A fine that works out to zero (on time, a child, or late only by closed days) adds no entry to the account.
+- The fine calculation is a pure policy function of the due date, the "until" day, the material, the replacement cost and the fines already charged on the loan. It does not read the loan's return date, so renewals can call it with the renewal day.
+
+**Account:**
+
+- The account is a history of entries per member, stored in the repository alongside members, copies and loans. The balance is derived from the entries, never stored separately.
+- Each entry records its kind (fine, manual charge, payment), amount, the day it was recorded (from the injected clock) and a reason. A fine entry also records its `loan_id` and the due date its lateness was counted from. A loan may have more than one fine entry (needed for renewals later).
+- The kinds are open-ended so a no-show fee can be added later without changing how the account works.
+- Balance = sum of charges − sum of payments. It is never negative.
+- A fine's reason names the returned copy, for example "late return of D1". A manual charge's reason is stored exactly as given and is not validated.
+
+**Amounts:**
+
+- An amount is acceptable only if it is a `Decimal` or an `int` (not a `bool`), finite, greater than zero, and has no more than 2 decimal places. Integers are converted to `Decimal`. Floats are rejected, not converted (money is never float).
+- `pay` additionally rejects any amount greater than the current balance, so paying when the balance is zero is always rejected.
+
+**Blocking:**
+
+- The borrowing blocking threshold is 10.00, the same for adults and children, and kept in circulation policy.
+- A member is blocked from borrowing when balance ≥ threshold.
+- Blocking is tied to the action, not stored on the member: the account holds no "blocked" flag. `checkout` compares the balance with the borrowing threshold.
+- `checkout` checks in this order: `MemberNotFound` → `AccountBlocked` → `CopyNotAvailable` → `LoanLimitReached`.
+
+**Modules touched:** domain models (an account entry record and its kind), circulation policy (daily rates, borrowing threshold, child exemption rule, fine calculation), errors (two new errors), repository (storing and listing a member's account entries), and the service (the three new methods, the fine on return, the block in checkout). The overdue report is not changed. The README's Concepts section should gain a short line on accounts, fines and blocking.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam: the public methods of `LendingService`.** Tests call `checkout`, `return_copy`, `balance`, `add_charge` and `pay`, and assert only on what those return or raise. They do not reach into the repository, the account entries or the policy functions.
+- **A good test describes behaviour a librarian would recognise.** For example: "a DVD returned a week late costs 6.00", not "the fine function returns X".
+- **Prior art:** the existing loan and catalogue tests, which use the shared `service` and `clock` fixtures (an in-memory repository and a `FixedClock` starting Monday 2 March 2026) and move the clock with `set`/`advance`. New tests should live in their own test module and reuse those fixtures. If they need a member with a balance, they set it up through `add_charge` or a late return.
+- **Scenarios to cover** (dates from the fixtures; DVD D1 replacement cost 12.00, book B1 replacement cost 18.00):
+  - DVD borrowed Mon 2 Mar (due Mon 9 Mar), returned Mon 16 Mar: 6 open days, balance 6.00.
+  - Book borrowed Mon 2 Mar (due Mon 23 Mar), returned Mon 30 Mar: 6 open days, balance 1.50.
+  - DVD borrowed Sat 7 Mar (due Sat 14 Mar), returned Sun 15 Mar: no fine. Returned Mon 16 Mar instead: 1.00.
+  - Returned on the due date: no fine.
+  - DVD returned 14 open days late: 12.00, not 14.00 (per-loan limit).
+  - A child's late DVD return: the loan is closed, the copy is shelved, and the balance is 0.
+  - `return_copy` still returns the closed loan with `returned_on` set when a fine is charged.
+  - `add_charge` and `pay` return the new balance; a part payment leaves the remainder; paying the full balance brings it to 0.
+  - A manual charge on a child counts towards their balance and can block them.
+  - Blocking: 10.00 owed means `checkout` raises `AccountBlocked` with the right `member_id`, `balance` and `threshold`; 9.99 owed means checkout succeeds; paying down below 10.00 unblocks.
+  - A blocked member asking for a copy that is on loan gets `AccountBlocked`, not `CopyNotAvailable`.
+  - `InvalidAmount` for: zero, negative, `Decimal("0.005")`, `Decimal("NaN")`, `Decimal("Infinity")`, a float, a payment above the balance, and any payment at balance 0. An `int` amount is accepted.
+  - `MemberNotFound` from `balance`, `add_charge` and `pay` for an unknown member.
+- The existing test suite must keep passing unchanged.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Blocking members who hold overdue copies (fines that are still building up do not count; see ADR-0001).
+- Waiving, reducing or refunding a charge.
+- A public method for reading account history or printing a statement.
+- Showing money on the overdue report.
+- **Holds** (future): a member owing 5.00 or more cannot place a hold. That will be refused with `AccountBlocked`, using a separate hold blocking threshold.
+- **No-show fee** (future): 1.00 charged when a copy set aside for a member's hold is not collected within the pickup time. It goes on the same account, counts towards the blocks, and is covered by the child exemption.
+- **Renewals** (future): renewing an overdue loan must charge the fine built up so far, calculated as if the copy were returned on the renewal day (same rates, per-loan limit and child exemption). Lateness after that counts from the new due date, and lateness before a renewal must never disappear.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- The vocabulary used here (Account, Charge, Fine, No-show fee, Manual charge, Payment, Balance, Blocking threshold, Blocked) is defined in `GLOSSARY.md`. In particular, "limit" means the number of open loans, so the money threshold is always a "blocking threshold".
+- ADR-0001 records why fines are charged when lateness is settled rather than accrued daily.
+- **Renewal decision already made:** the replacement-cost limit applies to the total of all fines on one loan, however many times it is renewed. For example, a 12.00 DVD renewed 10 days late (10.00) and then returned 10 days late again is charged only 2.00 more. The account entries already record `loan_id` so this can be enforced.
