+# Renewals
+
+Status: ready-for-agent
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Members often want to keep a book longer than its loan period. The system has no way to do that, so staff return the copy and check it straight back out. Each workaround closes the loan and opens a new one with a new loan id and a new borrowed date. The member's loan history then shows several short loans where there was really one, and the loan the member actually had can no longer be followed from start to finish. A workaround on an overdue copy also only settles the lateness by accident, as a side effect of the return.
+
+## Solution
+
+Staff can **renew** an open loan. The loan stays the same loan, with the same loan id and borrowed date, and gets a new due date: a full loan period counted from the day of the renewal. Only books can be renewed, at most twice per loan, and only from 5 days before the due date onwards. A member must be paid up (owe nothing) to renew. A loan can't be renewed while another member is waiting for its title. Renewing an overdue loan is allowed: it charges the fine built up so far, exactly as a return on that day would, and lateness after that counts from the new due date. A refused renewal changes nothing.
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a member, I want to renew a book I'm still reading, so that I can keep it without bringing it back.
+2. As a librarian, I want a renewal to keep the same loan, with its loan id and borrowed date, so that the member's loan history shows one loan, not a chain of returns and re-borrows.
+3. As a librarian, I want a renewed loan's new due date to be a full loan period from the day of the renewal, never on a Sunday, so that renewing works like borrowing again without breaking the history.
+4. As a librarian, I want to see how many times a loan has been renewed, so that I can tell a member how many renewals they have left.
+5. As a librarian, I want a loan that has never been renewed to show 0 renewals, so that existing loans need no special handling.
+6. As a librarian, I want a book loan renewed at most twice, so that a popular book can't be kept indefinitely.
+7. As a librarian, I want a third renewal refused, so that the limit is enforced at the desk.
+8. As a librarian, I want DVDs never to be renewable, so that the short DVD loan period means what it says.
+9. As a librarian, I want a member asking to renew a DVD told it can't be renewed, even if they also owe money, so that they aren't sent off to pay for a renewal that would be refused anyway.
+10. As a librarian, I want a renewal refused until 5 days before the due date, so that members can't renew on the day they borrow and double their loan period.
+11. As a member, I want to be told the first day I can renew, so that I know when to come back.
+12. As a librarian, I want a renewal on the first day of the window (e.g. Wed 18 Mar for a loan due Mon 23 Mar) granted, and one on the day before refused, so that the window is exact.
+13. As a librarian, I want only paid-up members to renew: any balance above 0 blocks renewal until they pay, so that renewals are a privilege of members in good standing.
+14. As a librarian, I want a member owing just 0.01 blocked from renewing, so that the threshold is exact.
+15. As a member, I want to be able to renew as soon as my payment brings my balance to 0, so that paying up unblocks me immediately.
+16. As a parent, I want my child held to the same paid-up rule as adults, so that blocking stays the same for every category.
+17. As a librarian, I want a member who has used up their renewals, or is asking too early, told so rather than told to pay, so that paying is only asked for when it would help.
+18. As a librarian, I want a renewal refused while any member is waiting for a copy of the title, so that the next person in the queue isn't kept waiting longer.
+19. As a librarian, I want a ready hold on the title not to stop a renewal, so that a member whose copy is already set aside doesn't hold up anyone else.
+20. As a librarian, I want an overdue loan to be renewable, so that a member who comes in late can keep the book instead of returning and re-borrowing it.
+21. As a librarian, I want renewing an overdue loan to charge the fine built up so far, calculated as if the copy were returned that day, so that renewing is never a way to escape a fine.
+22. As a librarian, I want lateness after an overdue renewal to count from the new due date, so that no day is charged twice.
+23. As a librarian, I want lateness before a renewal never to disappear, so that a late renewal followed by a late return charges both periods.
+24. As a librarian, I want all fines on one loan together never to exceed the title's replacement cost, however many times it is renewed, so that the cap means the same as for a single return.
+25. As a parent, I want my child never fined when renewing an overdue loan, so that the child exemption applies to renewals as it does to returns.
+26. As a librarian, I want renewing a loan on its due date to charge nothing, so that only lateness is fined.
+27. As a librarian, I want a member who owed nothing but renewed an overdue loan to owe the fine afterwards, and so be blocked from renewing again until they pay, so that the fine is treated like any other charge.
+28. As a librarian, I want a refused renewal to change nothing (no fine charged, the due date and renewal count unchanged), so that a failed request leaves no trace.
+29. As a librarian, I want renewing a loan that has already been returned refused, so that a closed loan can't be reopened.
+30. As a librarian, I want renewing an unknown loan to report that the loan doesn't exist, so that typos are caught.
+31. As a librarian, I want a renewal not to count against the member's loan limit, so that a member at their limit can still keep what they have.
+32. As a librarian, I want the overdue list and the overdue report to use a renewed loan's new due date, so that a renewed loan isn't reported as overdue.
+33. As a librarian, I want each fine from an overdue renewal recorded with a reason naming the copy, e.g. "late renewal of B1", so that I can explain it to the member.
+34. As a developer, I want every renewal refusal to raise one error type carrying the loan id and a reason, while the existing error types are reused where they fit, so that callers handle renewals uniformly.
+35. As a developer, I want the renewal limits, the renewal window and the renewal blocking threshold kept with the other circulation policy, so that the numbers live in one place.
+36. As a developer, I want `return_copy`, `checkout` and the existing loan reads to keep their signatures and behaviour, so that existing callers keep working.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+**Public interface (fixed; other code will call exactly this):**
+
+- `LendingService.renew(loan_id: str) -> Loan` renews the open loan in place and returns it, with the same `loan_id`.
+- `Loan` gains `renewals: int`, default `0`: how many times the loan has been renewed.
+- New error `RenewalRefused(LendingError)` for renewals that can't be granted. It carries `loan_id` and a short `reason`, both as attributes. It lives with the other domain errors.
+- Existing errors are reused where they fit: `LoanNotFound` for an unknown loan id, and `AccountBlocked` (with the renewal threshold) for a member who isn't paid up. `NoOpenLoan` is *not* used for a returned loan. It's keyed by copy and says the copy isn't on loan, which may be false by then because the copy can be out to someone else.
+
+**Checks, in this order.** Refusals that paying can't fix come before the balance check, so a member is only told to pay when paying would help. All checks run before anything changes.
+
+1. Unknown loan id → `LoanNotFound`.
+2. The loan is returned → `RenewalRefused` (e.g. "loan L0003 was returned on 2026-03-20").
+3. The title's material can't be renewed (its renewal limit is 0) → `RenewalRefused`, with a reason saying that material can't be renewed (e.g. "DVDs can't be renewed"), not "renewal limit reached".
+4. The loan has used its renewal limit → `RenewalRefused`.
+5. Too early: today is before the due date minus the renewal window → `RenewalRefused`, with a reason naming the first day renewal is possible (e.g. "loan L0001 can be renewed from 2026-03-18").
+6. Not paid up: balance ≥ the renewal blocking threshold of 0.01 → `AccountBlocked`, using the shared blocking check that `checkout` and `place_hold` use. Same for every member category. The balance is as it stands when the member asks, so a fine that is still building up on this loan doesn't count (ADR-0001).
+7. Any `WAITING` hold on the loan's title → `RenewalRefused`. `READY` holds don't count. This is answered by the repository's existing "waiting holds on a title" query.
+8. Backstop: the new due date would not be later than the current one → `RenewalRefused`. With today's numbers this can't happen (the window opens at most 5 days before the due date, and the loan period is 21 days), but it guards against future policy changes.
+
+**Granting a renewal**, in this order:
+
+- If the loan is overdue today, its lateness is settled first, using the same fine calculation as a return with "until" = today. Same daily rates, same child exemption, and the same replacement-cost cap on the total of all fines on this loan, with earlier fines from previous renewals counted as already charged. The result is a `FINE` entry with the loan's `loan_id`, `counted_from` = the due date before the renewal, and the reason "late renewal of <copy_id>". A fine of 0 adds no entry, as on return. The internal fine-charging step takes the entry's reason as a parameter so that returns keep "late return of <copy_id>".
+- `due_on` becomes the circulation policy due date for the title's material, counted from today: loan period in calendar days, moved to the next open day if it lands on a closed day.
+- `renewals` goes up by 1.
+- `loan_id`, `copy_id`, `member_id` and `borrowed_on` are unchanged. The copy stays `ON_LOAN`. Earlier due dates are not stored on the loan; their only trace is `counted_from` on any fine entry.
+
+**Policy** (with the other circulation numbers):
+
+- Renewal limit per material: book 2, DVD 0, kept as a per-material table like the loan periods and daily fine rates. A limit of 0 means the material can't be renewed.
+- Renewal window: 5 calendar days. A loan can be renewed from its due date minus 5 days (inclusive) onwards, with no upper bound, so an overdue loan is always inside the window. Example: due Mon 23 Mar, renewable from Wed 18 Mar; on Tue 17 Mar it's too early.
+- Renewal blocking threshold: 0.01, i.e. paid up means a balance of 0. Same for every category.
+
+**Unchanged:**
+
+- A renewal is not a checkout. It doesn't count against the loan limit, isn't checked against the borrowing blocking threshold (the renewal threshold is stricter anyway), and doesn't touch holds.
+- `checkout`, `return_copy`, `get_loan`, `open_loans`, `loan_history` and `overdue_loans` keep their signatures. Because the loan is changed in place, they and the overdue report all see the new due date.
+- Like `checkout` and `return_copy`, `renew` doesn't check whether the library is open on the day.
+- The README's Concepts section gains a **Renew** entry covering the rules above.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam: the public methods of `LendingService`. No new seams.**
+  - Renewing goes through `renew`, with results read from the returned `Loan` and `get_loan` (`due_on`, `renewals`, `loan_id`, `borrowed_on`).
+  - Fines charged by an overdue renewal are checked with `balance`. Balances are set up with `add_charge` and `pay`.
+  - After-effects are checked through the existing `return_copy`, `checkout`, `open_loans`, `loan_history` and `overdue_loans`. Holds are set up with `place_hold`, and copies set aside with `return_copy`.
+  - The overdue report gets one test through its existing entry point, as the catalogue tests already do.
+  - Refusals are checked by exception type, and each refusal test also asserts that the loan's `due_on` and `renewals` and the member's balance are unchanged.
+- **A good test describes behaviour a librarian would recognise,** for example "a book due Mon 23 Mar can be renewed on Wed 18 Mar and is then due Wed 8 Apr", not "the window function returns X". Tests assert only on what public methods return or raise. They don't reach into the repository, the account entries or the policy functions.
+- **Known gap:** the fine entry's reason ("late renewal of B1") and its `counted_from` aren't visible through any public method (a public account history is out of scope), so they are covered only indirectly through the amounts charged. The due-date backstop can't be triggered with today's policy numbers and isn't tested through the seam.
+- **Prior art:** the existing fines, blocking and holds tests. Use the shared `service` and `clock` fixtures: `FixedClock` starting Mon 2 Mar 2026; members `ann` (adult) and `kid` (child); book `978-1` (replacement cost 18.00) with copies B1 and B2; DVD `dvd-1` with the single copy D1. Register extra members, or add a cheap title, inside tests where needed. New tests live in their own module.
+- **Scenarios to cover** (`ann` borrows B1 on Mon 2 Mar, due Mon 23 Mar, unless stated):
+  - Window: renewing on Tue 17 Mar raises `RenewalRefused` and the reason names 2026-03-18. Renewing on Wed 18 Mar is granted: due Wed 8 Apr, `renewals` 1, same `loan_id`, `borrowed_on` still 2 Mar, no fine. Renewing on the borrow day is refused as too early.
+  - Limit: renewed on Wed 18 Mar (due Wed 8 Apr) and Fri 3 Apr (due Fri 24 Apr, `renewals` 2). A third attempt on Mon 20 Apr raises `RenewalRefused`, and still does when `ann` owes 5.00.
+  - DVD: `ann` borrows D1 (due Mon 9 Mar) and renews on Fri 6 Mar: `RenewalRefused`, also when she owes 5.00.
+  - Too early while owing 1.00 → `RenewalRefused`, not `AccountBlocked`.
+  - Paid up: `ann` owing 0.01 on Wed 18 Mar → `AccountBlocked` with threshold 0.01. After paying it off, the renewal is granted. `kid` owing 0.01 is blocked the same way.
+  - Holds: `ann` has B1, `bob` has B2, and `carol` holds `978-1`. `ann` renewing on Wed 18 Mar → `RenewalRefused`. After `bob` returns B2 and it is set aside for `carol` (`READY`), `ann`'s renewal is granted. `ann` owing 1.00 with `carol` waiting gets `AccountBlocked`.
+  - Overdue: renewing on Mon 30 Mar (6 open days late) is granted, the balance is 1.50, and the loan is due Mon 20 Apr. Returning on Mon 27 Apr (6 more open days late) brings the balance to 3.00. Renewing on the due date itself charges nothing and makes the loan due Mon 13 Apr.
+  - Overdue, then blocked: after the 30 Mar renewal (balance 1.50), renewing again on Wed 15 Apr → `AccountBlocked`. After paying 1.50, it's granted and the loan is due Wed 6 May.
+  - Cap across renewals: a book with replacement cost 2.00, renewed 6 open days late (1.50), then returned 6 open days late, is charged only 0.50 more (total 2.00).
+  - Child: `kid` renewing an overdue book is granted, and the balance stays 0.
+  - Refusal leaves no trace: an overdue renewal refused because of a waiting hold charges nothing, and `due_on` and `renewals` are unchanged.
+  - Closed and unknown: renewing a returned loan → `RenewalRefused`, and the loan stays returned. An unknown loan id → `LoanNotFound`.
+  - History and reads: after a renewal, `loan_history("ann")` has one loan for B1, and `open_loans` shows it with the new due date. A member at the loan limit can still renew.
+  - Overdue reads: after renewing on Wed 18 Mar (due Wed 8 Apr), `overdue_loans` on Mon 30 Mar doesn't include the loan, and the overdue report on Fri 10 Apr shows it 2 days overdue.
+  - A new loan has `renewals` 0.
+- The existing test suite must keep passing unchanged.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Members renewing for themselves (online, self-service), and any authorisation of who may renew: as elsewhere, the service has none.
+- Renewing several loans at once.
+- Different renewal limits or windows per member category.
+- Recording each renewal (date, previous due date) as its own history record. The loan keeps only the count and the current due date.
+- Recalling a renewed loan early when a hold is placed on its title after the renewal.
+- Refusing renewals on days the library is closed.
+- A public account history, which would make fine reasons and `counted_from` visible.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- Vocabulary: **Renew** is defined in `GLOSSARY.md`. The paid-up rule is the renewal **blocking threshold** (0.01). "Limit" means the number of open loans, so the cap on renewals is always the "renewal limit".
+- Decisions inherited from earlier specs:
+  - Holds spec, Further Notes (1): a waiting hold on the title refuses renewal, and a ready hold doesn't.
+  - Holds spec, Further Notes (2): paid-up only, a 0.01 threshold, `AccountBlocked`, with the balance taken when the member asks.
+  - Overdue-fines spec, Out of Scope and Further Notes: renewing an overdue loan charges the fine so far, lateness then counts from the new due date, and the replacement-cost cap applies to all fines on one loan.
+- That overdue-fines spec illustrates the cap with a 12.00 DVD renewed late. DVDs are now not renewable, so the rule is unchanged but only applies to books. The test above uses a cheap book instead.
+- ADR-0001 already anticipates renewals settling lateness ("and, once renewals exist, when an overdue loan is renewed"). No new ADR is needed: every choice here is a policy number or easy to reverse.
