@@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
+# Fines are charged when lateness ends, not accrued day by day
+
+A fine is put on a member's account only at the moment a late loan's lateness is settled: when the copy is returned (and, once renewals exist, when an overdue loan is renewed). Until then the fine that is building up is not part of the balance, so a member sitting on a copy three months overdue owes nothing for it yet and is not blocked by it. We chose this over a balance that grows daily with open overdue loans because it keeps the balance a plain sum of recorded charges and payments, makes every amount traceable to one dated entry, and means the balance never changes just because a day passed.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Blocking only reacts to charges already made. Stopping members who hold overdue copies from borrowing would be a separate rule, not a change to the balance.
+- The overdue report shows days overdue, not money, because no fine exists for an open loan.
